As you view this video, be sure to make notes of important information.

3
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:26,960
Feel free to pause, repeat, and resume the video whenever necessary.

4
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:31,960
Culturing Carrots Lesson 4.2, Performance Enhanced Plants.

5
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:36,960
Your teacher introduced Problem 424, The Complete Carrot.

6
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:43,960
As you consider how to propagate carrots, remember what tools and processes you used to propagate ferns earlier in the lesson.

7
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:48,960
This presentation will provide additional information about carrot culture.

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:01:01,800
This diagram gives a good overview of the basic steps involved in the carrot culture process you will use during Problem 424, The Complete Carrot.

9
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:08,800
Carrot plants have a taproot system which stores energy. This taproot is the vegetable many of us are accustomed to eating.

10
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:17,800
For tissue culture purposes, we will use the same structure to produce clones in an in vitro environment in order to complete the process of tissue culture.

11
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:25,380
The callus can take anywhere from two to six weeks to develop.

12
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:32,380
After that time, the callus can be transferred into a rooting medium containing oxens for root and shoot development.

13
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:36,380
After another six to eight weeks, tiny roots and shoots will form.

14
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:43,680
Plantlets will grow into clusters if they are too close in the medium.

15
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:53,680
To separate the plantlets, you will need to maintain a sterile environment and use disinfecting solution on the tools, just as you did during the initial culturing process.

16
00:01:53,680 --> 00:02:02,680
Later, as the plantlets reach a more mature size and form, you will transplant them, dividing plantlets again as necessary.

17
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:11,520
Record these vitals as you monitor the growth chamber during the development process.

18
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:19,230
These are the ingredients supporting plant cell formation and growth.

19
00:02:19,230 --> 00:02:28,230
As described previously, the culture media must supply all necessary requirements because very little plant tissue is used in tissue culture.

20
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:34,230
Sucrose is a sugar source which provides natural carbon for tissue growth.

21
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:41,230
Vitamin B is naturally produced in the vegetative growth of plants. It's necessary for plantlet health.

22
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:46,230
Oxens are plant hormones that promote cell division and enlargement.

23
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:53,660
Agar is a sugar substance used to gel liquids.

24
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:57,660
Remember how critical it is to maintain a safe and sterile workspace.

25
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:08,500
As a review, mark or highlight three key points in your notes that are important to remember from this presentation.

26
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:12,500
List two ideas or concepts that relate to previous knowledge.

27
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:18,500
List any questions you have about this topic. Discuss these questions with your instructor.

28
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:24,500
Keep any notes you have from this presentation organized and available for use throughout the course.

29
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:34,500
Through problem 424, the complete carrot, you will develop and write a protocol to insert a gene of interest in plants in order to understand how DNA can be cut,

30
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:40,500
replicated, and inserted into the genome of an organism for the development of agricultural production.

31
